Walmart says it is putting most of its capital toward higher-return areas centered on automation. Its strategy allocates the majority of capital to automation-focused areas such as eCommerce, supply chain, and store and club investments. [1] The filing reports that capital spending on supply chain, customer-facing initiatives, technology and other in the U.S. was $16,468 million in fiscal 2026, against $14,603 million in fiscal 2025. [2]

On the operational side, the company continues to invest in supply chain automation and in fulfillment and delivery capabilities. It runs 192 distribution facilities in the U.S. and 179 outside the U.S. [3] It also began combining the Sam's Club U.S. supply chain function with Walmart U.S. during fiscal 2026, in order to streamline operations and use its enterprise systems and infrastructure more widely. [3] The risk discussion likewise notes continued investment in supply chain automation and enhancements to support its omnichannel strategy. [4]

AI is also part of the picture. Walmart says it invests in AI to improve the efficiency of its supply chain, operations, management functions and talent recruitment and development, while cautioning that these are evolving technologies. [4]

The filing does not describe specific automation technologies or systems in detail.